ted.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (traceability)\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical\napproach; the strategy body already incorporates it and the decomposition\nfollows it:\n\n1. **No new flag.** The originally proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware`\n   flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed from\n   scope. The server work combines the existing `--artifacts-only` and\n   `--enable-workspaces` flags with a required `--workspace-store-uri`.\n2. **Operator work is a split-server topology, not a config toggle.** The\n   operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service\n   and HTTPRoute and configures artifact traffic routing from the main\n   tracking deployment — reflected in the operator deployment epic and the\n   routing branch epics.\n3. **SSAR detail de-specified.** Workspace authorization reuses the existing\n   auth plugin infrastructure; no epic dictates SelfSubjectAccessReview or\n   other Kubernetes API mechanics. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and\n   `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are unchanged.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate plugin workspace-resolution coupling to tracking-store initialization | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Decide the artifact traffic routing mechanism for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Measure per-request latency overhead of workspace validation | Investigation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E007 | Decide the telemetry channel for workspace access-denial audit events | Investigation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E008 | Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E009 | Verify plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context (no refactor) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009 | Refactor plugin workspace resolution into an independent initialization path | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E010 | Gateway-level artifact traffic routing in the operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E010 | Artifact traffic routing via tracking-server delegation in the operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E011 | Server-side artifact traffic delegation configuration | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nMain-plan epics: 8. Branch epics: 5 (not standalone Jira issues; attached to\ntheir gating Investigation as a plan document).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"E001 --> E003\" means E001 must\ncomplete before E003 starts).\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n  E001[\"E001 Investigate plugin coupling\"]\n  E002[\"E002 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only\"]\n  E003[\"E003 Operator split deployment\"]\n  E004[\"E004 Investigate routing mechanism\"]\n  E005[\"E005 Investigate latency overhead\"]\n  E006[\"E006 Membership decision caching\"]\n  E007[\"E007 Investigate audit channel\"]\n  E008[\"E008 Audit logging for denials\"]\n  BA_E009[\"BRANCH-A-E009 Verify plugin (outcome A)\"]\n  BB_E009[\"BRANCH-B-E009 Refactor plugin (outcome B)\"]\n  BA_E010[\"BRANCH-A-E010 Gateway routing (outcome A)\"]\n  BB_E010[\"BRANCH-B-E010 Delegation routing (outcome B)\"]\n  BB_E011[\"BRANCH-B-E011 Server delegation config (outcome B)\"]\n  E001 --> BA_E009\n  E001 --> BB_E009\n  E002 --> E005\n  E002 --> E006\n  E005 --> E006\n  E002 --> E008\n  E007 --> E008\n  E003 --> BA_E010\n  E003 --> BB_E010\n  E004 --> BA_E010\n  E004 --> BB_E010\n  E004 --> BB_E011\n```\n\nMain-plan critical path: E002 → E005 → E006 (length 3). Branch epics are\nexcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge (or deliberate non-edge) | Rule | Rationale |\n|------------------------------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rule 3 | The plugin-coupling investigation determines existence and scope of the plugin work: outcome A = verification only, outcome B = refactor. True gates recorded as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact` (rewrite / remove). Bounded (2 outcomes) → conditional branches. |\n| No edge E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (scoping) | The investigation's outcome changes only the plugin-side scope. The server-side approach (combine existing flags, initialize only the workspace provider) is fixed by the Staff Engineer input, so the server epic proceeds regardless of the outcome. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ E003 | Rules 14, 13 (exclusion), 15 | Different repositories with no shared build artifact. The artifacts-only Deployment's flag combination is a *configuration reference*, not an artifact the operator code builds against — the coupling is expressed as an AC on the operator epic (rendered command verified against the flag combination the server supports), never as a DAG edge. |\n| No edge E002 ↔ BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rules 14, 15 | Different repositories (server vs. plugin). The plugin refactor is internal to the plugin (its interface is preserved), and the server builds against the existing plugin interface. Integration of the two is an acceptance criterion on each epic, not a dependency. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 12 (analog) | The measured code path (per-request workspace validation) does not exist until the server work lands; the measurement cannot run earlier. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 3 | The caching epic is gated by the latency investigation: if overhead is within acceptable limits, caching is deferred to a follow-up release and the epic is removed (`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 12 | Caching extends the workspace-validation path built by the server work; the two touch the same request-handling surface, so the extension is serial. |\n| E002 → E008 | Rule 12 | Audit logging instruments the workspace-denial path produced by the server work; it builds on that surface rather than rebuilding it. |\n| E007 → E008 | Rule 3 | The audit implementation is gated by the telemetry-channel decision: if a channel other than server logs is selected, the epic's scope is rewritten (`gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`). |\n| E003 → BRANCH-A/B-E010 | Rules 12, 13 | Routing configuration targets the dedicated instance's Service and HTTPRoute, which the split-deployment work produces; the routing work builds against those resources. |\n| E004 → BRANCH-A/B-E010, E004 → BRANCH-B-E011 | Rule 3 | The routing-mechanism investigation selects the branch: outcome A = gateway-level routing (operator-only), outcome B = tracking-server delegation (operator + server config). True gates with `gate_failure_impact` (rewrite / remove). Bounded (2 outcomes) → conditional branches. |\n| No edge BRANCH-B-E010 ↔ BRANCH-B-E011 | Rule 13 (exclusion) | Within branch B, the operator's rendered configuration *references* the server's delegation surface — a configuration reference, expressed as an AC on the operator branch epic, not a DAG edge. |\n| No edge E003 ↔ E004, E003 ↔ E002 | Rules 14, 15 | Independent work in different repos/areas; the routing investigation is a design study runnable in parallel with the deployment work. |\n| No onboarding-chain edges | Rules 5–10 | All components are existing platform components with Konflux builds already in place; no repo-onboarding, license-validation, or konflux-onboarding epics exist in this decomposition. |\n\n## Open Question Disposition\n\n| Open question (from strategy) | Owner | Disposition |\n|-------------------------------|-------|-------------|\n| Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request (caching at launch vs. deferred) | PM/Engineering | Investigation epic (E005) measuring the implemented validation's overhead, plus a gated caching epic (E006, removed if overhead is acceptable). Not branched: a single gated epic whose existence varies is captured by `gated_by` + `remove`; an outcome with no epic cannot be represented as a branch file. |\n| How the operator configures artifact traffic routing; whether the tracking server needs a config change | MLflow Team | Investigation epic (E004) with two bounded outcomes → conditional branch epics (BRANCH-A-E010, BRANCH-B-E010, BRANCH-B-E011). |\n| Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability | MLflow Team | Investigation epic (E001) with two bounded outcomes → conditional branch epics (BRANCH-A-E009, BRANCH-B-E009). |\n| Telemetry channel for workspace access-denial audit events | PM/Security | Investigation epic (E007) — the gating question is a stakeholder decision (routed to a person), feeding a gated audit implementation epic (E008, rewritten if a non-server-log channel is selected). Not branched: one epic exists in every outcome; only its scope varies. |\n\n### Assumption disposition (implementation details vs. genuine unknowns)\n\n| Strategy assumption | Handling |\n|--------------------|----------|\n| `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` can be made composable without breaking either flag used individually | Implementation detail — acceptance criteria on the server epic (each flag individually preserves current behavior). |\n| Workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping without tracking stores | Genuine unknown — subsumed by the plugin-coupling investigation (E001) and its branches. |\n| Single-tenant deployments retain current behavior | Acceptance criteria on the server and operator epics. |\n| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes | Acceptance criterion / planning note on the operator epic; if structural chart changes turn out to be needed, that epic is re-split (see Health Warnings). |\n| Artifact request paths carry enough information to resolve workspace membership | Scope note and acceptance criterion on the server epic (S3 artifact store paths per the risk mitigation; limitations for non-standard artifact roots documented). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E002; BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 | Server implements validation; plugin branch epics co-enable the end-to-end behavior and therefore inherit P0 (see Health Warnings). |\n| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E002 | |\n| HLR-4: single-tenant / no-workspaces deployments unchanged | P0 | E002; E003 (constraining AC) | The operator's share is a no-change constraint on the opt-in topology (an AC), not P0-enabling work; E003 remains P1 with its P1 HLRs. |\n| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E003 | |\n| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | BRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E010 (+ BRANCH-B-E011) | Mechanism selected by the routing investigation's outcome. |\n| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E009 | Raised to P0 by priority inheritance — co-enables P0 HLR-2 (see Health Warnings). |\n| HLR-8: audit logging for workspace access denials | P2 | E008 | Split from the P0 server work rather than bundled: it is deferrable, its telemetry channel is an unresolved PM/Security decision, and it instruments (does not rebuild) the denial path — bundling would make P0 delivery contingent on a P2 decision. Serialized after the server work instead. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; none orphaned. No redundant/overlapping HLR pairs were\nfound (each HLR describes a distinct capability surface).\n\n## Conditional Decomposition (Branches)\n\nTwo investigations have bounded outcomes that change downstream epic structure:\n\n- **E001 (plugin coupling)** — Outcome A (workspace resolution initializes\n  without tracking stores): BRANCH-A-E009, verification-only. Outcome B\n  (resolution coupled to tracking-store initialization): BRANCH-B-E009,\n  refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path.\n- **E004 (routing mechanism)** — Outcome A (gateway-level routing suffices):\n  BRANCH-A-E010, operator-only routing. Outcome B (tracking-server delegation\n  required): BRANCH-B-E010 (operator routing configuration) plus\n  BRANCH-B-E011 (server-side delegation configuration support).\n\nBranch epics set `branch` and `gated_by` (which also appears in their\n`dependencies`), carry `gate_failure_impact`, and are documented as plan\nattachments on their gating Investigation — they are not standalone Jira\nissues and no main-plan epic depends on them.\n\nThe latency and audit-channel investigations intentionally use main-plan\ngated epics rather than branch files: in both cases exactly one epic exists in\nevery outcome and only its existence (remove) or scope (rewrite) varies, which\n`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` captures without a structural branch.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Priority inversion (strategy-internal).** HLR-7 is labeled P1, but plugin\n   workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context is required for P0\n   HLR-2's end-to-end acceptance (member requests served, non-member requests\n   denied). The plugin branch epics and their gating investigation therefore\n   inherit P0. Human review should confirm whether the strategy's P1 label\n   intended the plugin work to be deferrable.\n2. **Epic size — E002 (server).** Estimated at 1–2 sprints, which reaches\n   above the ~2-week epic guideline; the boundary is kept because flag\n   composition, workspace-provider initialization, and artifact-request\n   validation are one logical change over one surface (splitting would\n   double-touch the same request handlers). Re-split trigger: re-split along\n   sub-system lines (server initialization sequence vs. artifact\n   request-handling validation) if implementation finds the\n   initialization-sequence changes materially larger than estimated.\n3. **Epic size — E003 (operator deployment).** The strategy's 2–3 sprint\n   operator estimate is deliberately split into the deployment epic plus the\n   routing branch epics so each stays within the ~2-week guideline. Re-split\n   trigger: split the deployment epic further (Deployment/Service templates\n   vs. HTTPRoute/ingress configuration) if the Helm chart requires structural\n   changes beyond adding new templates.\n4. **Performance NFR has no target.** \"No latency target is specified\" — the\n   latency investigation produces the measurement and a PM/Engineering\n   threshold decision; until then \"acceptable overhead\" is undefined and any\n   caching assumption is a scope trap (handled by gating the caching epic).\n5. **Proposed acceptance criteria pending validation.** The strategy marks its\n   acceptance criteria as \"requires PM/Engineering validation\"; the\n   decomposition maps them onto epic ACs as drafted.\n6. **Caching appears twice with different framing** — as a risk mitigation\n   (\"implement short-TTL caching\") and as an open question (\"required at\n   launch or deferred?\"). Reconciled structurally as a gated, removable epic\n   so the decision is explicit rather than assumed.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store,\nauth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency\nwith uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":79302,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8865428d5e28a0d8","agent_id":"a654abfb6cba3ce32","session_id":"c475d54c-6029-4a26-8892-bfb5b0182313","uuid":"688ff8b4-6c18-48a5-b2a3-383b12104f8f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T19:29:35.277Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
